  known survivor  D467-benched-noun-arm-moves-behind-the-shipped-one — equivalent
      The two patterns are DISJOINT while the lookahead is present, so either order computes the identical function and no input can tell them apart. Measured rather than argued, in both directions: `YOUR_BENCH_SCALE` spells the literal `Benched Pokémon\.` and `YOUR_BENCHED_NOUN_SCALE` carries `(?!Pokémon\.)` at exactly that position, and both are `^…$`-anchored — so no string matches both, and the printed-ZERO case (where the shipped arm matches and then falls through its `per >= 1` guard) reaches the other pattern's lookahead and is refused there too, in BOTH orders. ⚠️ THE DECLARATION IS PREFERRED OVER DELETING THE ROW FOR D427's REASON: the day the lookahead is removed or weakened, the two patterns overlap, this mutation becomes observable, the row reports STALE-SURVIVOR and the run FAILS — which is a SECOND alarm on the same byte that `D467-benched-noun-loses-the-lookahead` covers from the other side. 🛑 AND IT IS THE REASON THE ARM IS PLACED AHEAD OF THE SHIPPED ONE AT ALL: at the house placement the lookahead's deletion is reader-INERT (D450) and unkillable, because these regexes are module-private and no suite can observe a pattern the reader never lets it reach. This row is what makes "the ORDER is legibility and the LOOKAHEAD is behaviour" a re-runnable claim instead of a comment.

